    "faq": [
      {
        "q": "How accurate is BMI as an individual health metric?",
        "a": "BMI is an epidemiological screening tool, not a clinical diagnostic measure. While it reliably tracks statistical health risks across large populations, BMI cannot distinguish between skeletal muscle mass, bone mineral density, fluid retention, and adipose tissue. For an individual, BMI is most informative when evaluated alongside waist circumference, body fat percentage, and metabolic laboratory markers."
      },
      {
        "q": "Why do Asian BMI thresholds differ from standard WHO ranges?",
        "a": "Landmark research published in The Lancet (2004) and WHO expert consultations established that Asian and South Asian populations typically exhibit higher percentages of body fat and visceral abdominal adiposity at lower body weights. Consequently, public-health action points for Asian adults are set lower: 23.0 kg/m² for increased cardiometabolic risk and 27.5 kg/m² for high risk, compared to standard 25.0 and 30.0 kg/m² cutoffs."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can athletes and muscular individuals have a high BMI?",
        "a": "Yes. Skeletal muscle is approximately 18% denser than adipose tissue. Bodybuilders, strength athletes, and highly active individuals frequently register a BMI above 25.0 or even 30.0 despite maintaining low body fat (often under 12–15%). In these cases, BMI overestimates body fatness, and direct measurements such as DEXA scans, skinfolds, or waist-to-height ratio provide far more accurate assessments."
      },
      {
        "q": "Why should you check waist-to-height ratio alongside BMI?",
        "a": "Waist-to-height ratio (WHtR) directly screens for central and visceral abdominal adiposity—fat stored deep around vital internal organs that strongly correlates with cardiovascular disease, insulin resistance, and hypertension. A widely validated clinical benchmark across genders and ethnicities is keeping your waist circumference under half your height (WHtR < 0.50)."
      },
      {
        "q": "What does a BMI score of 25.0 represent?",
        "a": "A BMI of 25.0 kg/m² marks the statistical transition point between the healthy weight range and the overweight category under WHO guidelines. It is not an immediate medical diagnosis, but rather a practical checkpoint to assess lifestyle habits, waist circumference, physical activity levels, and blood pressure with your healthcare provider."
      },
      {
        "q": "What is a healthy BMI reference range for adult men and women?",
        "a": "Under standard WHO guidelines, a BMI between 18.5 and 24.9 kg/m² represents the healthy reference range associated with the lowest statistical incidence of chronic lifestyle diseases. For Asian adults, the optimal range is 18.5 to 22.9 kg/m². Reference ranges apply equally to adult men and women, although women naturally maintain a higher baseline percentage of essential body fat."
      },
      {
        "q": "Why is visceral fat more hazardous than subcutaneous fat?",
        "a": "Visceral fat wraps around internal organs in the abdominal cavity and is metabolically active, secreting inflammatory cytokines and free fatty acids directly into the portal circulation. Subcutaneous fat, found beneath the skin on hips and limbs, serves primarily as passive energy storage. This is why abdominal waist measurements predict cardiometabolic risk better than total scale weight alone."
      },
      {
        "q": "How do aging and sarcopenia affect BMI interpretation in older adults?",
        "a": "As adults age past 60, natural loss of skeletal muscle (sarcopenia) often masks increases in body fat percentage, allowing an older individual to maintain a 'normal' BMI despite elevated adiposity. Geriatric health research often recognizes a slightly higher BMI range (23.0 to 27.0 kg/m²) as protective against nutritional frailty and mortality in older populations."
      },
      {
        "q": "How do BMR and TDEE formulas complement BMI screening?",
        "a": "While BMI screens body mass relative to height, Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) estimates baseline resting caloric burn using validated predictive models like Mifflin-St Jeor. Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) factors in physical activity level. Together, they provide actionable energy expenditure baselines for healthy weight maintenance or gradual caloric deficit planning."
      },
      {
        "q": "What is the difference between standard BMI and Real BMI screening?",
        "a": "Standard BMI calculates relative weight strictly using height and weight (kg/m²). Real BMI provides an educational screening suite that pairs your BMI score with complementary anthropometric formulas—such as waist-to-height ratio, BMR, and body fat percentage—to provide broader context without collecting or storing personal health data."
      }
